Remove `-march=native` from the vendored SealBot build (WP12-R Phase A, DESIGN_A §2.6).

The argument is LAW-15, not LAW-13. LAW-13 binds mantis's own committed build config, and
`vendor/external/` is gitignored, so a native vendor build would be the `make build.native`
situation rather than a violation. What LAW-15 binds is the INSTRUMENT: `sealbot_d5` has to
be the same opponent on two boxes or the bar is an opponent-instance artifact, which is
exactly falsified-register row F-20.

The mechanism, stated so a reader can check it rather than take it: the evaluator loads its
pattern table as `std::vector<double>` (`current/minimax_bot.cpp:41-42`) and the search then
SORTS candidate turns by those double scores (`current/engine/search.h:171-177`).
`-march=native` at `-O3` licenses FP contraction (FMA), so one host can compute a score one
ULP away from another's, reorder a tie, and return a different move. Removing the flag
removes the one host-specific-by-construction term.

Honest limit, recorded here rather than only in the design: this does NOT make the extension
bit-portable. Different compiler versions, a different libstdc++, or `-O3` alone can still
differ. Decision statement S-10 in `docs/contracts/eval_decision_run5.md` says so.

SECOND HUNK (WP12-R Phase A, REVIEW-impl must-fix 1): export the mate-distance constants.

`current/engine/search.h:178` ends each deepening iteration with

    if (std::abs(last_score) >= WIN_THRESHOLD) break;

and `current/engine/constants.h:51-52` defines `WIN_SCORE = 100000000.0`,
`WIN_THRESHOLD = WIN_SCORE - 1000.0` under the comment "mate-distance detection". That break is
a LEGITIMATE early exit: the engine stops deepening once it has proven a win, proven a loss, or
found a mate inside the threshold band. A host that drives `max_depth` and then checks
`last_depth` as a receipt has to be able to tell that exit apart from a silent time-out
truncation, or it rejects correct play — measured at review: `SealBotDepthError` in 6 of 6
games at run5's minted depth 5.

`last_score` already crosses the seam (`minimax_bot.cpp:92-94`); the THRESHOLD did not. Exporting
both constants is what lets the adapter read the engine's own condition verbatim instead of
re-typing `99999000.0` into Python, where it would rot silently the next time the pin moves.
Nothing else is touched: no behaviour changes, only two module attributes are added.

THIRD HUNK (R352 leg, 2026-09-14): release the GIL for the search.

`get_move` bound the whole call under the interpreter lock, so `eval.rung_concurrency` (R351,
minted 8) overlapped only the candidate's PUCT side while the eight sealbot searches ran one
at a time — the re-minted run7's terminal round spent ≈ 48 of its 96 minutes in a 288-game
rung whose sealbot moves are 0–3 s each (`RUN7_STAMP2_2026-09-14.md` §C). The extraction
(`extract_game_state`, Python objects) and the search (`engine.get_move(gs)`, pure C++ over the
wrapper's own `engine` — its transposition table is per instance, and the shared offset and
zobrist tables are built by the engine's constructor, under the GIL) were already separate, so
`py::gil_scoped_release` wraps the search alone. No move, score or depth receipt changes: the
same engine runs the same search on the same `gs`; only who else may run meanwhile does.

